# Tutorial 14 — Localization and Text Keys
# How to prepare your game for multiple languages using locale files and text keys.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_14_localization
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Planning to release in more than one language? Even if you're not sure yet, it's worth setting up now.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Retrofitting localization into a finished game is painful. Starting with it is almost free.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> What are text keys? -> section_textkeys
> Locale files -> section_locale_files
> Naming your keys -> section_naming
> Translation workflow -> section_workflow
> Common pitfalls -> section_pitfalls
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── What Are Text Keys ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_textkeys
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A {b}text key{/b} is a placeholder in your script that the engine replaces with the correct text at runtime, based on the player's language.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: {{ch1.greeting}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Instead of writing "Good morning!" directly, you write a key. The locale file maps that key to the actual text.
narrator: {b}en.locale:{/b} ch1.greeting = Good morning!
narrator: {b}ja.locale:{/b} ch1.greeting = おはようございます！
narrator: {b}de.locale:{/b} ch1.greeting = Guten Morgen!
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Same script, three languages. The VNS file never changes — only the locale files do.
> Next: Locale files -> section_locale_files
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Locale Files ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_locale_files
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Locale files live in your project's locale directory. One file per language.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/en.locale{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/ja.locale{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}locale/de.locale{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Each file is a simple key-value format:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.greeting = Good morning!{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.farewell = See you tomorrow.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.new_game = New Game{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.load = Load Game{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The engine loads the locale file matching the player's language setting. If a key is missing, it falls back to the default locale.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Always write your default locale file first (usually English). It's the reference that translators work from.
> Next: Naming keys -> section_naming
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Naming Your Keys ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_naming
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Key naming matters more than you think. A good naming scheme keeps things findable as your project grows.
narrator: {b}Pattern:{/b} category.context.description
narrator: {b}Examples:{/b}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch2.library.alex_warning{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}menu.settings.volume_label{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ui.confirm.yes{/color}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Don't use generic names like "text1" or "line_42". Your translator needs context to translate accurately.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A translator seeing "ch1.rooftop.lavender_greeting" knows exactly where this line appears and who says it.
> Next: Workflow -> section_workflow
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Translation Workflow ────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_workflow
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Here's the workflow that keeps things sane:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Write your script in the default language with text keys.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Fill out the default locale file (en.locale) with all the text.
narrator: {b}3.{/b} Send the locale file to translators — they only touch locale files, never VNS scripts.
narrator: {b}4.{/b} Translators create their locale file (ja.locale, de.locale, etc).
narrator: {b}5.{/b} Test each language in-game. Check for text overflow, missing keys, and tone.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The separation is the key insight: {i}scripts define structure, locale files define text{/i}. Your translators never need to understand VNS syntax.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: When you add new dialogue, add the key to all locale files. Mark untranslated keys with a prefix like "TODO:" so they're easy to find.
> Next: Pitfalls -> section_pitfalls
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Common Pitfalls ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_pitfalls
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Things that will bite you if you're not careful:
narrator: {b}Text length.{/b} German text is often 30% longer than English. Japanese is shorter. Test all languages for overflow.
narrator: {b}Concatenation.{/b} Don't build sentences from fragments — word order changes between languages. Use full sentences as keys.
narrator: {b}Hardcoded text.{/b} If you wrote "Hello!" directly in a VNS file, it can't be translated. Always use a key.
narrator: {b}Missing keys.{/b} If a key exists in en.locale but not ja.locale, the player sees the raw key name. Test for this.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And the cultural one:
narrator: {b}Humor and idioms don't translate literally.{/b} Give translators context notes — "this is sarcastic" or "this is a pun on X."
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Localization isn't just translation. It's making the game feel native in every language.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Localization cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}Text key:{/b} {{category.context.description}} — placeholder resolved at runtime.
narrator: {b}Locale file:{/b} locale/xx.locale — key = value pairs, one per language.
narrator: {b}Fallback:{/b} Missing keys fall back to the default locale.
narrator: {b}Key naming:{/b} category.context.description — descriptive and hierarchical.
narrator: {b}Workflow:{/b} Scripts use keys. Translators edit locale files. Never mix the two.
narrator: {b}Testing:{/b} Check text overflow, missing keys, and cultural fit for every language.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} Start with localization even for single-language games. It's free insurance.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: If you only remember one thing: never hardcode player-visible text. Always use a key.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's think about localization decisions. Pick a scenario:
> Translating a pun -> practice_pun
> Handling text overflow -> practice_overflow
> Organizing 500+ keys -> practice_organize

@label practice_pun
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Your character says "I'm reading a book about anti-gravity — it's impossible to put down!"
Lavender: That pun doesn't work in most other languages. What do you do?
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: You give the translator a note: "Pun — feel free to substitute a local equivalent. The tone is lighthearted."
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Good localization means the translator has creative freedom within the tone you set. Don't demand literal translation of wordplay.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_overflow
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Your English text fits perfectly in the dialogue box. Then you switch to German and — it overflows.
Lavender: "Wiedersehen" is a lot longer than "Bye."
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Solutions: design your UI with 30% extra space, or allow your text box to scroll, or work with translators to keep lines concise.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The best approach: test early. Don't wait until launch week to check your German build.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_organize
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: 500 keys. Three languages. How do you not lose your mind?
Lavender: Group by file: ch1.rooftop.*, ch1.library.*, ch2.*. Match your script structure.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use comments in your locale files to mark sections:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}# Chapter 1 — Rooftop Scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}ch1.rooftop.greeting = ...{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And run a script that checks all locale files have the same keys. Missing keys are silent bugs.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Cultural adaptation is the hardest part of localization — and the most important. Trust your translators.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Text overflow is the most common localization bug. Design for it from day one.
[else]
Lavender: Organization scales. A good naming convention at 50 keys still works at 5000.
[endif]
Lavender: Even if you ship in one language, text keys keep your code clean and your options open.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
